sections.body:
printf formats text; put polls transmit space then stores a byte. **LF** is line feed; **CR** is carriage return, inserted before LF for this terminal.

```c
static void put(char c) {
    while (!(USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_TXE_TXFNF)) {}
    USART2->TDR = (uint8_t)c;
}
int _write(int fd, const char *buf, int len) {
    (void)fd;
    for (int i=0; i<len; ++i) {
        if (buf[i]=='\n') put('\r');
        put(buf[i]);
    }
    return len;
}
```
USART2 base is 0x40004400; ISR offset 28=0x1C; TDR offset 40=0x28. LF is 10, CR13, TXE mask 128 at bit 7. Optimized _write has 22 instructions: setup/push at 0x080002B8–C0; CMP and BLT atC2–C4; return MOVS/POP atC6–C8; byte load and newline compare atCA–CE; CR polling/store atD0–D6; character reload/poll/store atD8–E0; increment/back-branch atE2–E4.

**Inlining** copies helper instructions into a caller; **hoisting** moves repeated calculations out of loops. **Aliasing** means expressions overlap storage; C character pointers may overlap many object types.

Constants are hoisted; put is inlined twice, with no BL. The exit is mid-listing; (void)fd emits nothing. Source/address order differ. Character aliasing permits reloading the byte after the peripheral store.

The **as-if rule** preserves observable behavior, not exact instructions. Cached nonvolatile reads can make -O0 work while -Os hangs. CMSIS __IO means volatile: required accesses, without atomic read-modify-write. Inspect widths, constants, offsets, conditions, prologue and return before counting cycles.

Return is 0x080002C6–0x080002C8. Complete optimized send loop:```asm
080002b8: b5f0 push {r4,r5,r6,r7,lr}
080002ba: 2300 movs r3,#0
080002bc: 2080 movs r0,#128
080002be: 250d movs r5,#13
080002c0: 4c09 ldr r4,[pc,#36]
080002c2: 4293 cmp r3,r2
080002c4: db01 blt 0x080002ca
080002c6: 0010 movs r0,r2
080002c8: bdf0 pop {r4,r5,r6,r7,pc}
080002ca: 5cce ldrb r6,[r1,r3]
080002cc: 2e0a cmp r6,#10
080002ce: d103 bne 0x080002d8
080002d0: 69e6 ldr r6,[r4,#28]
080002d2: 4206 tst r6,r0
080002d4: d0fc beq 0x080002d0
080002d6: 62a5 str r5,[r4,#40]
080002d8: 5cce ldrb r6,[r1,r3]
080002da: 69e7 ldr r7,[r4,#28]
080002dc: 4207 tst r7,r0
080002de: d0fc beq 0x080002da
080002e0: 62a6 str r6,[r4,#40]
080002e2: 3301 adds r3,#1
080002e4: e7ed b 0x080002c2
```
Both transmit blocks repeat a status load until bit 7 becomes one. The same LDRB instruction occurs before and after the possible carriage-return store. The loop’s signed BLT matches its signed int index and length.

**.text/.rodata** stay in Flash. **.data** has Flash **LMA** (load address) and SRAM **VMA** (run address, real here without MMU). **.bss** reserves SRAM without payload. **Linker symbols** name boundary addresses, not automatically stored address variables; startup copies/clears their ranges.

```c
extern uint32_t _sidata, _sdata, _edata, _sbss, _ebss;
void Reset_Handler(void) {
    uint32_t *src=&_sidata;
    for (uint32_t *dst=&_sdata; dst<&_edata;) *dst++=*src++;
    for (uint32_t *dst=&_sbss; dst<&_ebss;) *dst++=0;
    SystemInit();
__libc_init_array();
    (void)main();
    for (;;) {}
}
```
A **constructor** runs initialization before main. The **heap** serves dynamic allocation; the **stack** holds active execution state.

__libc_init_array runs C++/attribute constructors. The final loop catches main’s return without an OS. &_sdata is the boundary; _sdata reads its first data word. _edata-_sdata wrongly subtracts stored values.

```
MEMORY {
 FLASH(rx): ORIGIN=0x08000000,LENGTH=32K
 RAM(rw): ORIGIN=0x20000000,LENGTH=12K
}
_estack = ORIGIN(RAM) + LENGTH(RAM);
.text : { *(.text*) } >FLASH
.data : { _sdata=.; *(.data*) _edata=.; } >RAM AT>FLASH
_sidata = LOADADDR(.data);
.bss : { _sbss=.; *(.bss*) *(COMMON) _ebss=.; } >RAM
```
Dot is the location counter; >RAM selects run region; AT>FLASH selects load region. The real script includes Flash-base vectors, constants and runtime/alignment sections. Match C031C6 capacity: _estack computes 0x20003000.

| Lesson04 section | Bytes | Address |
|---|---:|---|
|.isr_vector|180|0x08000000|
|.text|5684|0x080000B4|
|.rodata|1652|0x080016E8|
|.data|100|0x20000000|
|.bss|372|0x20000064|
|._user_heap_stack|1536|0x20000218|

RAM use 100+372+1536=2008 B (16.34% of 12 KB). FLASH 180+5684+1652+4+4+100=7624 B (23.27% of 32 KB); the 4-byte runtime/alignment entries and .data load image count too. Prefer const for immutable arrays: flash-only. Zero-initialized bss saves flash. This build’s data source is 0x08001D64; runtime data ends 0x20000064, bss ends/heap begins 0x200001D8.

The nonzero C witness can be declared `int magic = 0xC0FFEE;`; the padded print 0x00C0FFEE is the same value.

48 MHz HSI resets divided by 4, so core starts 12 MHz. A **wait state** allows Flash another clock interval. Set/read back latency before removing the divider; confirm readiness:

```c
FLASH->ACR = (FLASH->ACR & ~FLASH_ACR_LATENCY) | FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_0;
while ((FLASH->ACR & FLASH_ACR_LATENCY) != FLASH_ACR_LATENCY_0) {}
RCC->CR &= ~RCC_CR_HSIDIV;
while (!(RCC->CR & RCC_CR_HSIRDY)) {}
SystemCoreClock = 48000000UL;
```
FLASH ACR at 0x40022000 has LATENCY bits 2:0, PRFTEN bit 8, ICEN bit 9, ICRST bit 11, PROGEMPTY bit 16, DBG_SWEN bit 18. RCC CR HSIDIV bits 13:11 selects oscillator division, HSIRDY bit 10 indicates readiness. One wait state is used here. Increasing frequency before latency can break instruction fetch. Decrease latency only after decreasing clock. C0 needs no PLL/source switch for this change; F446 later requires those additional steps.

**Retargeting** connects library operations to board code: _write sends serial bytes; _sbrk supplies dynamic-allocation heap space.

_write adds CR before LF, sends each byte via uart2_putc polling/store, and returns len; it serves printf/puts/putchar/fprintf. retarget.c _sbrk uses the bss-to-stack gap for printf’s allocated buffer. A bad heap can silence output while main runs. Source estimates newlib-nano printf≈1.4 KB on 32 KB; AVR’s4 KB target used fifteen custom formatters.

**Read-modify-write** edits a register copy then stores it: ODR |=/&=~ /^= use LDR, ORR/BIC/EOR, STR. If main reads 0 before ISR sets PA6=0x40, main’s PA5 store 0x20 erases PA6. **volatile** preserves accesses but not this sequence.

A **command register** requests actions: BSRR ones set/reset selected pins; zeros do nothing, avoiding stale ODR copies.

BSRR lower 16 bits set, upper 16 reset. PA5 high=0x00000020; low=0x00200000; BRR bit 5=0x20 also resets. Each update is one store, atomic against ordinary interrupt interleaving.

```c
GPIOA->BSRR = 1u << 5;
GPIOA->BSRR = 1u << 21;
GPIOB->BSRR = ((uint32_t)(uint8_t)~leds << 16) | leds;
```
For leds=0b00000101, set mask 0005 selectsPB0/PB2 and eight-bit inverse00FA resetsPB1/PB3–7, producingBSRR 0x00FA0005. Bits forPB8–15 arezero, preserving them. Narrowing the inverse before shifting prevents resetting unrelated upper pins. This replaces a dangerous whole-port read-modify-write with one command; it does not resolve two writers issuing contradictory commands to the same pin.

Register diagrams call the lower BSRR command bits BS0–BS15 and upper bits BR0–BR15. Thus BS5 is bit 5, BR5 bit 21; BS7 bit 7 and BR7 bit 23. BRR names its own low-half reset commands BR0–BR15. LCKR has lock bits LCK0–LCK15 and the lock-key bit LCKK at 16.

**Bounce** is rapid contact switching before settling. Active-low: released 1, pressed 0. Wokwi default models 10–100 transitions/~1 ms; "bounce":"0" disables it. Count accepted states, not raw edges.

**Debouncing** accepts an unchanged observation after a quiet interval. raw=latest logical press; stable=accepted state; held_ms=unchanged duration; changes=raw transitions. Logical pressed 1 corresponds to physical pin 0. d-> accesses pointer-held state.

A polls every 1 ms; accept after 20 unchanged samples. State: uint8_t stable/raw, uint16_t held_ms, uint32_t changes, initially 0 (logical released).

```c
static int debounce_step(debounce_t *d, uint8_t raw) {
    if (raw != d->raw) {
        d->raw=raw; d->held_ms=0; d->changes++;
        return 0;
    }
    if (d->held_ms < DEBOUNCE_MS) {
        d->held_ms++;
        if (d->held_ms==DEBOUNCE_MS && raw!=d->stable) {
            d->stable=raw;
            return raw ? +1 : -1;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```
Return+1=press, -1=release,0=no event. A raw change resets timing. Hand trace with threshold 3:

|ms|raw|held|stable|result|
|---|---|---|---|---|
|1|0|1|0|none|
|2|0|2|0|none|
|3|1|0|0|change|
|4|0|0|0|change|
|5|1|0|0|change|
|6|1|1|0|none|
|7|1|2|0|none|
|8|1|3|1|press|
|9|1|3|1|none|

changes increments at 3/4/5; acceptance is delayed until 8. Real threshold 20 trades latency for confidence. Sampling 1 kHz misses microsecond-scale bounce transitions; a small changes count does not prove a clean contact. A115200-baud character costs about 87 µs with 10 serial bits, so blocking printf also delays polls. Human buttons often tolerate this; short pulses may vanish between polls.

An **ISR** checks its shared-vector line, acknowledges flags, increments b_edges, then returns. The counter is volatile, aligned to its access boundary, with one writer.

```c
void EXTI0_1_IRQHandler(void) {
    uint32_t mask=1u<<1;
    if ((EXTI->RPR1|EXTI->FPR1)&mask) {
        EXTI->RPR1=mask;
        EXTI->FPR1=mask;
        b_edges++;
    }
}
```
RPR/FPR are write1-to-clear: assign the mask. |= would read all currently pending lines and write their ones back, discarding unrelated events. Forgetting the clear leaves the request asserted; the core repeatedly re-enters and main’s heartbeat/output stops. This 14-instruction handler uses only already-stacked R1–R3, reads offsets 12/16, and returnsBX LR with hardware EXC_RETURN. Source estimates a visit around 1 µs at 48 MHz; not a universal measured latency.

A **strong definition** overrides an exactly named/cased weak default. nm **T** means defined code; **W** means weak, showing which target survived linking.

Misspelled EXTI0_1_IRQhandler builds with 0 warnings: image 7156→7120 B loses the 36-byte handler; nm changesT toW. First press reaches Default_Handler. Banner compares vector 21 with the default to detect the missing handler.

main reads b_edges once; a changed count restarts a 20 ms quiet timer. After 20 ms with no new edge, sample the pin and accept its settled state. This counter is safe to read without masking because it is one aligned 32-bit value with one writer, but volatile remains necessary. Waiting 20 ms inside the ISR would block equal/lower-priority work. Keep the ISR to acknowledge/count/return.

Circuit roles: PA6 AF1 TIM3_CH1=servo waveform; PA7 AF4 TIM14_CH1=echo measurement; PA8=HC-SR04 trigger; PB0–PB7=proximity bar; PA5/LD4=heartbeat; PA2/PA3 AF1=serial. **AF** selects a peripheral; **CH1** means channel 1. Sensor power=5 V/GND; follow actual device voltage limits, not simulator acceptance.

**SysTick** counts down independently of main. Cortex-M base 0xE000E010 is ARM-defined.

|Offset|Register|Fields|
|---|---|---|
|00|CTRL/SYST_CSR|COUNTFLAG bit 16, CLKSOURCE bit 2, TICKINT bit 1, ENABLE0|
|04|LOAD/SYST_RVR|RELOAD23:0|
|08|VAL/SYST_CVR|CURRENT23:0; write clears|
|0C|CALIB/SYST_CALIB|NOREF bit 31, SKEW bit 30, TENMS23:0; read-only|

**COUNTFLAG** records at least one wrap, not how many; pending exceptions likewise coalesce. Count milliseconds with promptly serviced ticks incrementing a software counter.

24 bits permit 2^24 clocks, about 350 ms at 48 MHz. Polling COUNTFLAG remembers one wrap, not a queue:90 characters at 115200 baud can block roughly 8 ms and lose seven elapsed tick counts.

```c
static volatile uint32_t ms_ticks;
void SysTick_Handler(void) { ms_ticks++; }
/* main initialization; check nonzero return as an invalid period */
SysTick_Config(SystemCoreClock/1000u);
```
CMSIS checks ticks-1 against 0xFFFFFF, writesLOAD=ticks-1=47999=0xBB7F, sets SysTick priority 3, clearsVAL, setsCTRL=CLKSOURCE|TICKINT|ENABLE=7. No explicit flag clear is needed in SysTick_Handler: exception acceptance acknowledges its request. Normal UART printing can be interrupted, so ticks continue. However, a pending exception is not an unlimited counter; masking or higher-priority work across several periods can coalesce ticks. A late tick is safe only while another wrap does not overrun service.

uint32_t milliseconds wrap after 2^32 ms≈49.7 days. Test elapsed unsigned differences, `millis()-start >= period`, instead of absolute deadlines. Modular subtraction survives one wrap when the elapsed interval fits the counter range; it cannot detect multiple full wraps. Reading ms_ticks needs no lock for one aligned word with a single writer, but requires volatile.

```c
uint32_t now=millis();
if (now-ping_at>=100) { ping_at+=100; ranger_ping(); }
if (now-report_at>=1000) { report_at+=1000; report(); }
if (now-beat_at>=500) { beat_at+=500; heartbeat(); }
```
Adding fixed periods preserves the schedule after 3 ms delay; assigningnow accumulates drift. **Cooperative** jobs must return quickly: one 50 ms job delays all main-loop jobs 50 ms. RTOS adds preemption.

Add each hexadecimal table offset to the block base for its address.

**Preload** holds the next setting; a **shadow register** supplies the current period, avoiding mid-period changes. PSC is buffered; ARR needs ARPE, CCR1 OC1PE. **UG** transfers buffered settings through an update before starting:

```c
TIM3->PSC=47;
TIM3->ARR=19999;
TIM3->EGR=TIM_EGR_UG;
TIM3->SR=~TIM_SR_UIF;
TIM3->DIER|=TIM_DIER_UIE;
```
UG also sets UIF, so clear it before enabling update interrupts. Without UG the first period may use PSC 0:20000/48 MHz≈0.417 ms instead of 20 ms (the source rounds 0.4 ms). ARR took effect immediately in that setup because ARPE had not yet been set.

Timer flags are **write0-to-clear**, unlike EXTI write1-to-clear. `TIM3->SR=~TIM_SR_UIF` writes 0xFFFFFFFE, clearing UIF while ones preserve other flags. MOVS#2/NEGS/STR can form it. `SR=TIM_SR_UIF` leaves UIF set, clears others and causes re-entry. Check UIF first.

**PWM** (pulse-width modulation) repeats high/low intervals. **Duty fraction**=high time/period. Servo position mainly follows high-pulse duration; compute it as well as duty.

PWM mode 1 usesOC1M=0110: output high whileCNT<CCR1. CCMR1=0x68 setsOC1M bits 6:4=110 andOC1PE3=1; CCER=1 enablesCC1E; CR1=0x81 sets ARPE/CEN. Configure GPIO alternate mode/AF as well. With 1 µs ticks, CCR1 is pulse width inµs. Preload applies duty changes at a frame boundary.

Nominal servo pulses repeat each 20 ms:1000 µs one end, 1500 centre, 2000 other end. Real endpoint ranges vary; Arduino Servo uses 544–2400 µs for 0–180°, while the cited Wokwi documentation specifies no range. Lab sweeps CCR1 to observe endpoints rather than assuming calibration.

Datasheet AF depends on the pin:TIM3_CH1 isAF1 onPA6/PB4/PC6, AF12 onPB6, AF11 onPB7. TIM14_CH1 isAF4 onPA7, AF0 onPB1. The source cross-checks ST-generated pin data in Zephyr hal_stm32. WrongAF yields a counting timer and dead servo output.

```c
uint32_t now=TIM3->CCR1, want=servo_target_us;
if (want>now+20) now+=20;
else if (want+20<now) now-=20;
else now=want;
TIM3->CCR1=now;
```
20 µs per 20 ms frame is 1000 µs/s: a rate limiter glides toward the target. The update ISR adjusts the target pulse once per consumed frame; hardware generates each edge independently of CPU execution.

Nominal 1500 µs=1.5 ms center;1000/2000 µs=1.0/2.0 ms endpoints. Arduino’s544–2400 µs defaults do not guarantee physical/simulated calibration.
